-- title:
--   Zero scheme of a section of a line bundle is locally principal
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and $M$ an object of $X.\mathrm{Modules}$ which is invertible in the sense of the predicate `Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible`: every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ for which the pullback of $M$ along the inclusion $U \to X$ admits an isomorphism with the unit sheaf of modules on $U$. Let $s : \mathbb{1}_{X.\mathrm{Modules}} \to M$ be a morphism from the monoidal unit, i.e. a global section of $M$. For an open $U$ and a morphism $\varphi : M|_U \to \mathbb{1}_{U.\mathrm{Modules}}$, `Scheme.Modules.coeff s U \varphi` is the element of $\Gamma(X, U)$ obtained by applying $\varphi$ over $\top$ to the restriction of $s$ and transporting along the canonical identification of $\Gamma(U,\top)$ with $\Gamma(X,U)$; `coeffIdeal s U` is the ideal of $\Gamma(X,U)$ spanned by all such coefficients, and `zeroSchemeIdeal s` is the infimum, in $X.\mathrm{IdealSheafData}$, of those ideal sheaf data $J$ with $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}\,s\,U \le J.\mathrm{ideal}\,U$ for every affine open $U$. The conclusion is the conjunction of two assertions: first, for every affine open $V$ of $X$ one has $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}\,s\,V \le (\mathrm{zeroSchemeIdeal}\,s).\mathrm{ideal}\,V$, so that this infimum is itself a member of the family it is the infimum of; second, for every affine open $U$ and every isomorphism $\tau : M|_U \cong \mathbb{1}_{U.\mathrm{Modules}}$, the ideal $(\mathrm{zeroSchemeIdeal}\,s).\mathrm{ideal}\,U$ equals the principal ideal generated by $\mathrm{coeff}\,s\,U\,\tau_{\mathrm{hom}}$ and also equals $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}\,s\,U$.
--
--   This is the basic property of the zero scheme $Z(s)$ of a global section of a line bundle: its ideal sheaf is the least quasi-coherent ideal sheaf containing all coefficient ideals of $s$, and on any affine open trivialising $M$ it is the principal ideal generated by the image of $s$ under the trivialisation. It underlies the treatment of relative effective Cartier divisors and their comparison with line bundles in the relative Picard functor, where the zero schemes of sections are identified fibrewise and after twisting by pullbacks.
